#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138282 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138558 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3108f0ab-17b1-5241-93d1-97658cf22601" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Three Collinear Points"}] :question/id #uuid "5b1ee9ef-da39-5f7a-abb5-98149a765b1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2565" :question/problem "Let $C$, $D$ and $E$ be three collinear points such that $D$ is between $C$ and $E$. If $CE = 8\\,\\text{cm}$ and $CD = 6\\,\\text{cm}$, find the length of $\\overline{DE}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/30/304a0de1777efb414a2504e22d53c1d15a5a0299196a7a9103a02491ff12e4c6.png)\n\nThe segment addition postulate states that\n\n$$\nCE = CD + DE\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the formula above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCE &= CD + DE \\\\\n8 &= 6 + DE \\\\\n8 - 6 &= DE \\\\\n2 &= DE\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nDE = 2 \\, \\text{cm}\n$$\n\n."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138283 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138559 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7679cf4-427a-59ed-8b0f-d483af20ede8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between More Than Three Collinear Points"}] :question/id #uuid "cabd7daa-4f22-56ca-8b26-0791857e8eae" :question/math-academy-id "e-2566" :question/problem "Given the diagram shown below, what is the measure of $\\overline{BC}$?\n\n![](images/54/543c7c26c7d56d8ff10f4ece71b74bb5f04edf59fddb6f2c0df549848000abaf.png)" :question/worked-solution "The segment addition postulate can be applied to a set of more than three collinear points. In the given situation, it states that\n\n$$\nAD = AB + BC +CD\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the equation above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAD &= AB + BC + CD \\\\\n21 &= 5 + BC + 12 \\\\\n21 &= 17 + BC \\\\\n21 - 17 &= BC \\\\\nBC &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138284 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138560 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c48d15-4749-5c47-bc97-a5584216cf2e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of an Unknown Quantity Using Collinearity"}] :question/id #uuid "6df54266-d6d6-54f0-8b61-4b5eabf0fcbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-2567" :question/problem "Given the diagram shown below, what is the value of $x$?\n\n![](images/3f/3fe1e3d1e6f89af1050d0437379a811eabb19212be36f0fecd91ddfaca2594ee.png)" :question/worked-solution "By the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD = AB + BC +CD\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x$, we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\nAD=30, \\quad AB=x+5, \\quad BC=13, \\quad CD=5x\n$$\n\nand then solve the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAD &= AB + BC + CD \\\\\n30 &= (x + 5) + 13 + 5x \\\\\n30 &= 6x + 18 \\\\\n6x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138285 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138561 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da4a8391-42b6-510c-8cb4-acbbbe7d1585" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of an Unknown Quantity Using Collinearity and Midpoints"}] :question/id #uuid "fc35d08c-518a-5f03-8e5f-627d58c572d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-2568" :question/problem "In the diagram below, the length of the line segment $\\overline{AD}$ is $8d+12$, $AB = 4d+4$, and $C$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{BD}$. Express the length of $\\overline{AC}$ in terms of $d$.\n\n![](images/46/468fd25032d1a36e1658331053a33652cef7ff9ce3d9c95b729d6a7b7ebf1672.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, note that according to the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD = AB + BD\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBD &= AD - AB \\\\\n&= (8d + 12) - (4d + 4) \\\\\n&= 4d + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $C$ is the midpoint of $BD$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= \\frac{1}{2}BD \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(4d + 8) \\\\\n&= 2d + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= AB + BC \\\\\n&= (4d + 4) + (2d + 4) \\\\\n&= 6d + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138561 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da4a8391-42b6-510c-8cb4-acbbbe7d1585" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of an Unknown Quantity Using Collinearity and Midpoints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93e6ba35-344d-5675-8ced-062323c83944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6d + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135923} {:answer/id #uuid "a49bd6f6-2e96-5aa1-9e59-2350f885e2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2d + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135924} {:answer/id #uuid "de69d80d-2fce-5c76-a458-cbe80f054cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6d + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135925} {:answer/id #uuid "2341be70-1972-5001-b5d2-6fa5f432827e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3d + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135926} {:answer/id #uuid "7653b9e2-65df-5de6-b25a-b214dbedff6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3d + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135927}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c9283bb4-6ecf-5de6-856e-2ac2b30d7426" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135922}] :question/id #uuid "01a9d857-28c1-509b-bab9-73d8c8e051ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-52726" :question/problem "![](images/46/468fd25032d1a36e1658331053a33652cef7ff9ce3d9c95b729d6a7b7ebf1672.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the length of the line segment $AB^(―)$ is $5d + 6$, and $AD = 11d + 12$. If $C$ is the midpoint of $BD^(―)$, find the length of $CD^(―)$."}]}
